WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.439
My name is Greg Sands. I'm a partner at Sutter Hill Ventures

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:19.169
and was a graduate of the GSB class of 1994. And I have with

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:23.239
me on the panel two CEO's of wonderful entrepreneurial

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:25.259
companies. I'll let them introduce themselves, but they've

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:31.230
got great stories to tell and I think also a lot of

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.569
information to share about thinking about when venture

00:00:33.589 --> 00:00:38.989
capital makes sense, when venture capital doesn't make

00:00:39.009 --> 00:00:42.069
sense, the process of evolving and growing a business, as

00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:47.739
well as the process of raising money and what are some of

00:00:47.759 --> 00:00:50.759
the ways that you make sure that you tee yourself up for

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:52.259
success when you're in that environment.

00:00:52.259 --> 00:00:56.829
So, let me introduce Matthew Monahan who's the CEO of

00:00:56.849 --> 00:00:59.349
Inflection which is a leading people search company that he'

00:00:59.349 --> 00:01:02.349
ll describe in a bit more detail, and Terry Austin who's the

00:01:02.349 --> 00:01:11.290
CEO of Guardian Analytics which is a anti - fraud solution

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.629
for the online banking industry. I happen to be a board

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:20.900
member at both companies, but they're the best two companies

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.670
you're likely to find and I'm thrilled to be involved with

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.420
them both. I think it's also helpful that one is more

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.200
consumer internet based and one is enterprise focus based.

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:32.670
So Matthew, do you want to talk first?

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.670
Sure.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.420
Just tell them a little bit about the company and about

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.170
yourself so that the audience has a little bit of context?

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.120
Yeah. So, I sold a business prior to this. It was a consumer

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.510
based eBooks business that was only about five people when

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.890
we sold it and then moved into Inflection. This was in 2006.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:54.390
And so, I started it with my brother. He was at Harvard

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:55.980
doing this from his dorm room.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.469
And essentially, the thesis is that a lot of the public

00:01:59.489 --> 00:02:01.869
record systems moving online right now and there's a ton of

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:05.140
data being digitized and moving onto the internet that hasn'

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:08.729
t been affectively organized. And so, we saw an opportunity

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:14.199
here to build a platform to not only bring the data in, but

00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:16.899
then also package it for consumers through a variety of

00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:20.289
channels whether it's the search engines, and the social

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.310
networks, and so forth.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.280
So today, we're up to about 100 employees. We have two main

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.139
brands - the first is called archives.com which is a family

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:31.769
tree, family history website that allows you to search your

00:02:31.789 --> 00:02:35.089
ancestors. And the second is called PeopleSmart.com which is

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:39.089
a search engine for people. And the main goal there is, "How

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:41.859
do you solve people search in a privacy friendly way?" And

00:02:41.859 --> 00:02:44.859
then we have a bunch of other vertical brands that we are

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:47.719
adding data and rolling out including identity protection,

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:49.859
employment screening.

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:53.669
We raised money in the middle of 2010, so it took us about 3

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:56.789
1/2 years before we decided to raise our series A deal. And

00:02:56.789 --> 00:02:59.449
so, up until that point we bootstrapped the company. It's

00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:02.039
been profitable since we started which is something that we'

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.619
re very focused on and very proud of. And it's a phenomenal

00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:11.089
team of a bunch of great people. And we're just over here on

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:12.109
[inaudible]

00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:14.419
Great Matthew, great background. And there's a whole bunch

00:03:14.419 --> 00:03:19.149
there that I think is interesting and are great lessons in

00:03:19.169 --> 00:03:24.129
that process about starting a business, evolving a business,

00:03:24.149 --> 00:03:26.799
coming up with the strategic framework for what's really a

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:30.759
good business and a defensible business that we can get into

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.539
over time as well.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.369
So Terry, do you want to do the same thing?

00:03:34.369 --> 00:03:35.369
Sure.

00:03:35.369 --> 00:03:38.119
Talk a little bit about the company and about yourself so

00:03:38.119 --> 00:03:39.369
people have some broad context?

00:03:39.369 --> 00:03:42.669
Sure. Absolutely. I'm Terry Osmond, CEO of Guardian

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:47.779
Analytics. I've been CEO for two years. The company's been

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:51.229
around for about 5 1/2 - almost six years. So, I joined

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:55.569
later. I joined after the company had done kind of a seed

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.519
followed by an "A" round. Though we've labeled it

00:03:58.539 --> 00:04:01.609
differently I'll just use that.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:07.289
And the company had a version 1 product and they had about

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.269
six or seven customers using the product. So, there were

00:04:10.289 --> 00:04:14.709
certain prove points that had been established and I'd been

00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:18.199
there for about two years and did a couple of transitions of

00:04:18.219 --> 00:04:20.879
the company. We're now on version 4 of the product. So, we'

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:24.139
ve introduced some pretty significant revs. And the most

00:04:24.159 --> 00:04:27.459
recent one was to move to a Sass architecture to deliver

00:04:27.479 --> 00:04:30.910
this product in a much more scalable and fast growing

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:31.930
manner.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:37.959
So, the essential solution is fraud prevention and online

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:42.280
banking as Greg mentioned. We solve a really massive, and

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.410
growing, and vexing issue. Internet banking's been around

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:49.719
for a long time. It's evolved quite a bit. There's a new

00:04:49.719 --> 00:04:53.810
generation of internet banking that's upon us with mobile

00:04:53.829 --> 00:04:57.550
banking, with all sorts of different paying mechanisms being

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:01.889
integrated into the online banking system. And one of the

00:05:01.909 --> 00:05:04.740
things that doesn't get talked about much is that fraud has

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.819
grown and almost outpaced the innovation of online banking

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:08.839
itself.

00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:12.319
And the protections that have been in place to date have not

00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:15.810
really been effective to stop online fraud. It keeps getting

00:05:15.829 --> 00:05:18.740
bigger. More money gets stolen. The criminals are more

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:22.870
embolden than ever and there's very little repercussion for

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:26.879
the criminal that gets caught. They tend to be overseas.

00:05:26.899 --> 00:05:29.889
There's not much jurisdiction that can affect them. They

00:05:29.909 --> 00:05:32.409
just move on to the next victim. And the traditional

00:05:32.409 --> 00:05:34.659
techniques of either trying to protect your computer or

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:37.019
mobile device with anti - virus protection or anti - malware

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:42.810
protection have failed. It's a finger in the tyke against a

00:05:42.829 --> 00:05:46.360
tsunami that's hitting them with malware provocation. And

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.170
authentication techniques are really an attempt to put a

00:05:49.189 --> 00:05:53.069
really strong lock on the door. Any of the most

00:05:53.089 --> 00:05:56.040
sophisticated multi - factor authentication techniques have

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.439
also failed because they made the assumption that once

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:03.480
somebody successful logs in and passes whatever test you

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.759
give them, then you can trust them.

00:06:05.779 --> 00:06:09.410
And the fact is that all of the malware that's loaded on all

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.939
these machines and mobile devices is designed to defeat

00:06:11.959 --> 00:06:18.670
authentication. And once it's defeated and the criminals' in

00:06:18.689 --> 00:06:21.689
they can do all sorts of things to steal money and extract

00:06:21.689 --> 00:06:23.189
value out of the financial system.

00:06:23.189 --> 00:06:25.689
So, we take a radically different approach to the problem.

00:06:25.689 --> 00:06:27.939
We assume that the end point's compromised and that

00:06:27.939 --> 00:06:30.189
authentication's defeated and we use all of the information

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:32.689
that a user creates on the interaction with the online

00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:35.389
banking system to create a very specific, individualized

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:40.620
behavioral profile of that user. And from that we can

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:44.129
actually very precisely and very accurately detect when

00:06:44.149 --> 00:06:49.120
someone's logging in that's not a legitimate user. And we're

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:51.639
now deployed at about 45 banks and credit unions throughout

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:55.350
the country using the system we're targeting to sort of long

00:06:55.370 --> 00:07:00.779
tail the banking industry. There are about 17,000 financial

00:07:00.799 --> 00:07:03.799
institutions that are a target market in the U.S. And that's

00:07:03.799 --> 00:07:05.629
a kind of essential solution.

00:07:05.649 --> 00:07:15.269
Okay. So, what I'd love to do is to frame a little bit how I

00:07:15.289 --> 00:07:19.449
think about entrepreneurial companies, and what I look for,

00:07:19.469 --> 00:07:22.189
and what's interesting to me. But I don't actually want the

00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:28.670
whole discussion to be about fundraising and about venture

00:07:28.689 --> 00:07:33.379
capital because a big part of that story is really about

00:07:33.399 --> 00:07:38.120
identifying good businesses and good people.

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:41.290
And so, you know, to me the kernels in there that are

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:48.629
interesting is focusing on partly how I'm evaluating, you

00:07:48.649 --> 00:07:53.060
know, our great people, great CEO's and entrepreneurs so

00:07:53.079 --> 00:07:57.610
that you can chart your course in an appropriate way and

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:03.290
partly what are really good businesses. And I think in both

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.889
cases there are some really interesting anecdotes about

00:08:06.909 --> 00:08:10.180
transitioning the business to be a better business. And

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:14.089
that's what I'm most interested in. But there is a little

00:08:14.109 --> 00:08:18.800
bit of framework over that and we'll sort of bounce back and

00:08:18.819 --> 00:08:22.680
forth with I think a couple of these factors.

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:27.129
So, at Sutter Hill Ventures we've been in the venture

00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:31.610
capital business in Silicon Valley for 48 years. You know,

00:08:31.629 --> 00:08:36.519
by virtue of doing that I think we are not - and don't try

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:41.519
to specifically be - sector specialists in the sense that I'

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:45.250
m a Sass guy, or I'm a consumer internet guy, or the like. I

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:49.200
think my mandate is basically anything that touches software

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:53.210
and comes from software and the like, you know, you have to

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:58.519
evolve with the times. And you ultimately do that by:

00:08:58.539 --> 00:09:00.990
Figuring out how to be in business with great people.

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.080
How to get into really good businesses.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:08.070
And the way I like to say that is some of my partners will

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.590
say, "So, we're micro - ergonomic." Meaning, you know, not

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:15.250
thematic, right? You can think about, you know, there are

00:09:15.269 --> 00:09:16.740
firms that are

00:09:16.759 --> 00:09:20.580
[inaudible] firms that are consumer only firms just like you

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.279
look at the marketing of mutual funds and some will say, "

00:09:25.299 --> 00:09:28.049
Hey, we're the science and technology team." What we want to

00:09:28.049 --> 00:09:30.549
be is the really good business with really great readers.

00:09:30.549 --> 00:09:33.299
And you do that by looking - from my perspective - at the

00:09:33.299 --> 00:09:36.559
company's unit analysis, not the sector. Right?

00:09:36.579 --> 00:09:39.419
So, I know people will come in and say, "Man! I've really

00:09:39.419 --> 00:09:40.669
got to be in the

00:09:40.669 --> 00:09:42.669
[inaudible] search business. I'm going to go and interview

00:09:42.669 --> 00:09:44.919
six companies." When people search business I'm going to

00:09:44.919 --> 00:09:48.330
figure out which of those is the best because I've already

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.840
determined that it's a sector that I want to be in. There

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.230
are people that do it - it's not what I do. If you try to

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.480
initiate that conversation at my partnership you get thrown

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.480
out of the room.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.710
And so, people would say, you know, "Let's talk about a

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.830
company", right? I mean, you can go meet with six companies.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.850
Talk about one that you think is a good example. It's a

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.850
little bit like thinking of it as the case study method of,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:17.269
you know, studying here at the GSB. But those basically do

00:10:17.289 --> 00:10:20.120
come down to, you know, fundamental economics of the

00:10:20.139 --> 00:10:21.470
business

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:24.279
[inaudible]. Are you in a growing sector? Is there an

00:10:24.299 --> 00:10:26.230
incumbent leader that you're displacing? Are you

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.730
evangelizing a new market? Do you have the meek and

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:31.419
proprietary technology or are there business advantages

00:10:31.439 --> 00:10:37.799
that, you know, that you either have or are pretty clear

00:10:37.819 --> 00:10:42.970
that you can build over time. And you know, the fundamental

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:49.370
framework for evaluating that is risk and reward.

00:10:49.389 --> 00:10:53.919
And so, lots of people come and say, "Hey, I'm building this

00:10:53.939 --> 00:10:57.509
marketplace and you know, once I have the buyers and sellers

00:10:57.529 --> 00:11:00.620
I'll have this great defensible advantage." But of course,

00:11:00.639 --> 00:11:06.149
various entry before you have them are various entry for you

00:11:06.169 --> 00:11:09.549
too, right? So you know, one of my partners always says, "

00:11:09.569 --> 00:11:12.589
That's great. That's a ham and eggs business model. If I had

00:11:12.589 --> 00:11:16.089
ham and if I had eggs I'd have ham and eggs. But you start

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:18.589
out with neither and it really doesn't do you a whole lot of

00:11:18.589 --> 00:11:19.589
good."

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:26.750
You know, the people side of it is really very nuanced,

00:11:26.769 --> 00:11:30.559
right? We're all just making our own judgments and our own

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:35.909
assessments for, you know, some things that are general and

00:11:35.929 --> 00:11:38.639
some things that are specific to the opportunity. And I

00:11:38.659 --> 00:11:47.090
think the right place to start when I think about it is that

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:50.350
in my partnership the single greatest compliment that you

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:54.110
can give someone is that they're intellectually honest, you

00:11:54.129 --> 00:11:58.129
know? And both of these guys met that task.

00:11:58.149 --> 00:12:00.870
The question is are you evaluating data? And are you living

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.519
by the data? And are you learning every day? And do you have

00:12:04.539 --> 00:12:08.819
a strategy? And is it working? Are you unafraid to call it

00:12:08.839 --> 00:12:13.250
and to change? And you know, if somebody can do that that's

00:12:13.269 --> 00:12:18.590
the fundamental process. Another way of saying it is

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:23.429
starting a company is a great truth telling device, right?

00:12:23.449 --> 00:12:26.419
You know, the market doesn't lie and if it doesn't like what

00:12:26.419 --> 00:12:31.110
you're doing you've got to do something different. And you

00:12:31.129 --> 00:12:34.329
know, there are plenty of people out there who either only

00:12:34.349 --> 00:12:37.990
want to hear good news, only want to raise the next round so

00:12:38.009 --> 00:12:42.460
that they can keep running the company. And so, I think

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:46.500
that's just, you know, the basic piece.

00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:51.539
It's also the case that leadership matters, right? You see

00:12:51.559 --> 00:12:56.840
it in, you know, all kinds of circumstances, but ultimately

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:59.710
entrepreneurial companies should not succeed. And the only

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:04.490
way that they succeed is by small bands of great people

00:13:04.509 --> 00:13:07.899
taking on giants and accomplishing things that shouldn't be

00:13:07.919 --> 00:13:11.439
able to be done. And in order to do that you have to

00:13:11.459 --> 00:13:14.289
galvanize resources that you don't deserve of better quality

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:18.679
people than ought to be, you know, drawn into these small

00:13:18.699 --> 00:13:24.639
companies, more capital and resources than, you now, a

00:13:24.659 --> 00:13:29.579
traditional financial investor would be willing to provide,

00:13:29.599 --> 00:13:32.299
partners in distribution channels where people are taking

00:13:32.319 --> 00:13:38.029
big bets on you that they shouldn't in some logical

00:13:38.049 --> 00:13:39.049
construct.

00:13:39.069 --> 00:13:41.769
And so, I think that notice when it says, "Intellectual

00:13:41.789 --> 00:13:46.000
honesty, leadership to galvanize and draw those kind of

00:13:46.019 --> 00:13:52.829
resources, enough self confidence and humility to be willing

00:13:52.849 --> 00:13:57.949
to consistently hire people who are better than you are in a

00:13:57.969 --> 00:14:04.220
specific domain." And then there are some really complicated

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.809
pieces about, "How do you hire someone in a domain that you

00:14:09.829 --> 00:14:11.549
don't know that well?"

00:14:11.569 --> 00:14:16.409
And the easy way I say that is I think for a lot of people

00:14:16.429 --> 00:14:17.679
coming out of business school

00:14:17.679 --> 00:14:21.620
[inaudible] "I got to hire a CEO, hire a VP of Marketing,

00:14:21.639 --> 00:14:25.620
hire a product manager" is a relatively straight forward

00:14:25.639 --> 00:14:28.639
thing to do. Hiring a VP of Engineering when you're not an

00:14:28.639 --> 00:14:33.259
engineer - that's hard, right? Hiring a VP of Sales actually

00:14:33.279 --> 00:14:36.169
when you don't have sales experience yourself - really hard

00:14:36.189 --> 00:14:39.709
cause sales people know how to sell.

00:14:39.729 --> 00:14:44.590
And it's really hard. And to me those are the two biggest

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:47.319
challenges. And most people if they're going to become CEO's

00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:50.179
at some point have to go from being really knowledgeable in

00:14:50.199 --> 00:14:54.149
a function to being a general manager managing functions

00:14:54.169 --> 00:14:56.539
where they don't know as much as the people that they're

00:14:56.539 --> 00:14:57.789
hiring and are working for.

00:14:57.789 --> 00:15:02.069
And so, that judgment that says, "Boy, this is someone that

00:15:02.089 --> 00:15:05.169
can figure that out, who knows what they know, knows what

00:15:05.189 --> 00:15:09.090
they don't know, is learning quickly enough the stuff that

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:15.709
they don't know." And you know, that to me is sort of the

00:15:15.729 --> 00:15:17.590
central framework.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:24.750
So you know, I think with that I'd actually like to turn to

00:15:24.769 --> 00:15:33.340
Matthew. And I'll give a little bit about what I saw in the

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.899
business when I saw him, but then ask him to tell the story

00:15:36.919 --> 00:15:41.049
of the beginning and the evolution of that business.

00:15:41.069 --> 00:15:47.899
So first, I'll say I was totally impressed with Matthew the

00:15:47.919 --> 00:15:51.799
minute I met him. And so, as you can see these guys are

00:15:51.819 --> 00:15:54.100
different ages, they've got different backgrounds.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:55.120
[Informal Talk]

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:14.079
I actually try and look through someone's age, or exact

00:16:14.099 --> 00:16:18.980
experience, and the like when I'm trying to infer qualities

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:25.470
and capabilities. And I think some of the things that I

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.980
referenced were just crystal clear in Matthew, right?

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.899
Dedication to the company and to the task., commitment to

00:16:33.919 --> 00:16:37.100
being that driven, running more experience and learning

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.699
faster - including failing faster - than anybody else, had

00:16:41.719 --> 00:16:46.519
already demonstrated in spite of being a relatively young

00:16:46.539 --> 00:16:50.079
CEO that he'd been able to hire people more experienced in

00:16:50.099 --> 00:16:54.750
certain disciplines and be able to attract talent seeing as

00:16:54.769 --> 00:16:58.289
that's always one of the challenges for a relatively young

00:16:58.309 --> 00:17:05.369
CEO, and an incredible sense of self knowledge, and

00:17:05.389 --> 00:17:11.000
humility, and willingness to learn from all places, right?

00:17:11.019 --> 00:17:16.299
Not that I or the other investors are, you know,

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:21.539
pontificating from on high. We're one upon many. He's out

00:17:21.559 --> 00:17:24.720
talking to business partners and talking to other CEO's, and

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:28.299
just learning, and picking it up. So, if there were ever a

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:31.569
time where you said, "This is a guy who is great and getting

00:17:31.569 --> 00:17:33.819
better every day", you know, this is the guy.

00:17:33.819 --> 00:17:37.000
The other thing that I think is interesting and points out

00:17:37.019 --> 00:17:42.519
just a little bit about the venture process is that there

00:17:42.539 --> 00:17:48.029
were a couple of things that gave me a little bit of insight

00:17:48.049 --> 00:17:51.049
into the business, right? So, one of which was I had just

00:17:51.049 --> 00:17:53.960
done a big genealogy project. So, I had some personal

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:54.980
knowledge of the space.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:58.029
The second is that I had done a bunch of work in

00:17:58.049 --> 00:18:02.839
monetization, and lead generation, and ad targeting. And

00:18:02.859 --> 00:18:05.149
while they're using that technology stack for a very

00:18:05.149 --> 00:18:08.329
different purpose, it was a technology stack that I was

00:18:08.349 --> 00:18:09.349
relatively familiar with.

00:18:09.349 --> 00:18:11.839
So, I think for anybody that you're trying to pitch the

00:18:11.839 --> 00:18:14.309
business to - they're having one or two of those access

00:18:14.309 --> 00:18:17.589
points that take things that might be a little mysterious

00:18:17.609 --> 00:18:20.680
and make them really easily understandable is a big

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:22.160
difference.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:25.920
So, that was my orientation coming into the business. Now,

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:29.159
Matthew can tell you how the business started, how he

00:18:29.179 --> 00:18:31.929
actually grew it to that point, and some of the decisions

00:18:31.929 --> 00:18:33.179
they made along the way.

00:18:33.179 --> 00:18:36.179
Cool. Thank you for the kind words. So, when we started the

00:18:36.179 --> 00:18:40.920
business we had about $75,000 - 100,000 in seed capital from

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:44.230
the proceeds of the last business. So, that's some of the

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:47.250
context on self - funding it. But we were able to do that

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.259
and self - fund that business from there over the few years.

00:18:50.279 --> 00:18:53.720
One of the key things that I went into at Silicon Valley - I

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:59.259
moved out here in early 2007. I had very strong skepticism

00:18:59.279 --> 00:19:02.460
of VC's and I actually would encourage you all to have

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:05.230
strong skepticism of the VC's - except for Greg. No. But in

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:09.909
all seriousness I think that, you know, you hear a lot of

00:19:09.929 --> 00:19:12.429
horror stories. You hear a lot of stories of entrepreneurs

00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:15.179
saying, "Hey, you know, don't raise money if you don't have

00:19:15.179 --> 00:19:17.179
to", or "I got booted from my company", or something like

00:19:17.179 --> 00:19:18.179
that.

00:19:18.179 --> 00:19:21.679
And so, I came in with my guards up. I was very fearful of

00:19:21.679 --> 00:19:24.679
even going down that path or that process. And we had a

00:19:24.679 --> 00:19:27.679
business model where if you could figure out how to make $1.

00:19:27.679 --> 00:19:31.329
10 for every dollar that you invested for promoting the

00:19:31.349 --> 00:19:35.710
products that you could scale it relatively well. And I also

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.629
had the distinct advantage that I had a co - founder that

00:19:38.649 --> 00:19:41.899
between us we kind of knew how to build the site, put the

00:19:41.909 --> 00:19:44.659
Ecommerce engine together, and do the marketing without

00:19:44.679 --> 00:19:48.009
needing too much other talent. Although, you know,

00:19:48.029 --> 00:19:50.670
Engineering talent was the first folks to hire.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.509
So, I would echo from a entrepreneur perspective what Greg

00:19:53.529 --> 00:19:57.950
was saying about the people mattering the most. I think its

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:01.779
mattered the most to us in terms of the team that we brought

00:20:01.799 --> 00:20:04.549
on board and it's been a huge factor consideration in terms

00:20:04.549 --> 00:20:07.669
of how we selected the VC's - Greg and then also Josh from

00:20:07.669 --> 00:20:09.059
Matrix Partners.

00:20:09.079 --> 00:20:13.369
And so, my personal philosophy is just kind of copy what

00:20:13.389 --> 00:20:16.879
other people figured out. So, on a hiring front we do -

00:20:16.899 --> 00:20:20.519
smart and gets things done. Those are kind of the two

00:20:20.539 --> 00:20:23.289
criteria in terms of how we think about position. Part of

00:20:23.289 --> 00:20:27.599
getting things done means that they fit in culturally. And

00:20:27.619 --> 00:20:32.329
so, you know, no asshole rule. We want to make sure that

00:20:32.349 --> 00:20:35.920
people are, you know, going to fit into the company and do

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.559
well. But that formula - Google's repeated it. There's been

00:20:39.579 --> 00:20:43.579
a lot of engineering blogs that have talked about that -

00:20:43.599 --> 00:20:44.920
smart and gets things done.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.440
And then on the partner side - which we consider our

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:51.250
investors to be partners - it's Warren Buffet's model which

00:20:51.269 --> 00:20:54.269
is, "People that you know, people that you like and people

00:20:54.289 --> 00:20:57.809
that you trust". And I just think that's the best wisdom I'

00:20:57.829 --> 00:21:01.269
ve been able to find on how to select partners is you've got

00:21:01.269 --> 00:21:04.460
to get to know them, you've got to like them - and Greg's a

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:07.460
very likable guy - and you've got to trust them. And I think

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:09.960
integrity was always very critical to us in terms of

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:13.039
selecting an investment partner partly because we were a

00:21:13.059 --> 00:21:16.220
little bit skeptical of bringing on money as entrepreneurs.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:24.849
So, how we went down the process was we basically friended a

00:21:24.869 --> 00:21:25.869
venture capitalist...

00:21:25.869 --> 00:21:28.970
Actually, back up about two years...

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:29.990
Sure.

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.990
cause I think the main part of the interesting thing that I

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:36.240
want these guys to hear is how did you go for three years

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:37.240
without raising any money...

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:38.240
Yeah.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.250
cause I think that's actually a big part of it too.

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:45.289
So in 2007, the gentleman that had purchased our prior

00:21:45.309 --> 00:21:48.559
business, he was in North Carolina and he made an

00:21:48.579 --> 00:21:52.690
introduction to his roommate back from Harvard Business

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:56.619
School. And one of the recurring things - I didn't go to

00:21:56.639 --> 00:21:58.889
business school. I actually dropped out of undergrad. But

00:21:58.889 --> 00:22:03.159
one of the things that has happened since I've been kind of

00:22:03.179 --> 00:22:05.309
trying to scale the organization and everything else is

00:22:05.309 --> 00:22:07.589
those of you that are in business school - the connections

00:22:07.589 --> 00:22:10.970
that you guys are getting cannot be underestimated. Do not

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:14.460
underestimate them. Hugely important in so many ways. I can'

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:15.490
t reinforce that enough.

00:22:15.509 --> 00:22:21.720
Be nice to everybody.

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:24.740
And on that front - just a side tangent here - but if you're

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:27.740
not an engineer and you need to hire an engineer, or you're

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:30.990
not a salesperson and you need to hire a VP of Sales, I

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:33.990
think part of the way to solve that is through weak ties.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.990
And there's been a lot of studies that, you know, the more

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:39.490
weak ties that you have, the more successful you will

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:42.740
become. And I think having a lot of weak ties with people in

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:45.599
diverse interest groups which business school is a great

00:22:45.619 --> 00:22:48.269
breeding ground for that in many ways in terms of university

00:22:48.269 --> 00:22:51.299
environment. You can build a lot of those weak ties and you

00:22:51.319 --> 00:22:53.819
never know when those phone calls or those contacts are

00:22:53.819 --> 00:22:54.819
going to be useful.

00:22:54.819 --> 00:22:58.309
But that aside, the first thing I did when I started the

00:22:58.329 --> 00:23:03.019
business was I had a mentor that had kind of introduced me

00:23:03.039 --> 00:23:06.799
to internet marketing long ago, and then the gentleman who

00:23:06.819 --> 00:23:09.430
had bought our prior business - we brought them in as

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:13.339
advisors. And they had some stock in the company, but they

00:23:13.359 --> 00:23:19.460
didn't have any board seats. We didn't have a board. There

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:22.980
was none of that. But they kind of serviced us as kind of a

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:25.730
functional board if you will because we didn't have a formal

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:28.230
kind of people who were giving us feedback, and supervising

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.480
us as the entrepreneurs and as the co - founders.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.980
And that worked extremely well. I think that, you know,

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:35.230
getting advisors as people you trust - whether it's a

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.230
professor, whether it's a friend, whatever - but getting

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.480
people that you respect and feel are knowledgeable in

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:41.980
certain areas to advise you on the business is really

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:44.679
important. And they introduced us to a gentleman by the name

00:23:44.679 --> 00:23:48.779
of Jason Green who is a venture capitalist over at Emergence

00:23:48.799 --> 00:23:52.009
Capital. And Jason and Rick - one of our advisors - had been

00:23:52.029 --> 00:23:53.329
roommates.

00:23:53.349 --> 00:23:57.769
Jason we purely met from a, "Hey, this is an entrepreneur."

00:23:57.789 --> 00:24:00.849
"Hey, this is a guy I trust. You guys should get to know

00:24:00.849 --> 00:24:03.599
each other because you're new to the Bay area." And what

00:24:03.599 --> 00:24:07.099
Jason did was he invited me to one of the D.C. events that

00:24:07.099 --> 00:24:09.849
Emergence Capital puts on where, you know, you have this big

00:24:09.849 --> 00:24:12.349
atrium and all these people, you know, cocktails, and food,

00:24:12.349 --> 00:24:14.599
and so forth. And it was there that we met our first

00:24:14.599 --> 00:24:15.599
executive.

00:24:15.599 --> 00:24:18.849
And so, this was the path that we took, but basically it was

00:24:18.849 --> 00:24:21.599
there that I met a gentleman by the name of John

00:24:21.599 --> 00:24:24.079
[inaudible] who would run Match.com from '97 - 2000. He had

00:24:24.079 --> 00:24:28.839
a very lustrous career in internet, helped a few companies

00:24:28.859 --> 00:24:29.859
go public.

00:24:29.859 --> 00:24:35.299
And we basically maintained very low salaries. For the first

00:24:35.319 --> 00:24:37.970
I think nine months we didn't really make anything and then

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:41.220
after that it was like $30,000 a year. We were putting all

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:44.220
of the money into executive hiring. And John was one of the

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:46.470
first executives we brought on board that also brought

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:50.399
another executive who is named Donald Lanneret. And those

00:24:50.419 --> 00:24:54.220
guys made a humongous impact on our business because what

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:56.859
they did is they took a lot of the business development,

00:24:56.859 --> 00:24:59.769
they took a lot of the operations completely out of our

00:24:59.789 --> 00:25:06.559
mindshare - our being Brian and I - so we could focus on the

00:25:06.579 --> 00:25:07.579
products and marketing.

00:25:07.579 --> 00:25:10.079
So, for us getting the products and marketing nailed really

00:25:10.079 --> 00:25:13.079
is all that mattered. And I think one of the most important

00:25:13.079 --> 00:25:15.579
lessons that I've learned in terms of that experience is

00:25:15.579 --> 00:25:18.329
anything you can do to reduce your plate and continue to

00:25:18.329 --> 00:25:21.579
focus on the few things that you really have to get done for

00:25:21.579 --> 00:25:24.389
everything else to develop, take advantage of those

00:25:24.409 --> 00:25:29.069
opportunities and don't underestimate them. Try not to do

00:25:29.089 --> 00:25:31.849
everything. As an entrepreneur you want to do everything.

00:25:31.869 --> 00:25:34.619
Try not to do everything. Try to make sure that you're

00:25:34.619 --> 00:25:35.940
nailing the really important stuff.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:39.210
And you know, if I draw that theme from a couple of other

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:42.429
examples that I've seen and the thing that I think Matthew

00:25:42.449 --> 00:25:46.349
and Brian did so well is think of themselves out of the gate

00:25:46.369 --> 00:25:50.029
as founder and VP of X, right?

00:25:50.049 --> 00:25:56.210
So, the way entrepreneurial companies succeed and especially

00:25:56.230 --> 00:26:01.899
really don't require a ton of capital is one as much,

00:26:01.919 --> 00:26:05.109
probably more focused on getting stuff done than on being

00:26:05.129 --> 00:26:09.460
smart. And even when there is a team of founders, there is

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:15.929
no room for overhead, right? So you know, picking out those

00:26:15.949 --> 00:26:18.389
two most important things and saying, "They're too important

00:26:18.389 --> 00:26:20.889
for them to be done by anybody but the founders" .

00:26:20.889 --> 00:26:23.389
So, I can think of another example - Merced Systems - where

00:26:23.389 --> 00:26:25.819
I'm involved. Basically one of them said, "I got product"

00:26:25.819 --> 00:26:28.819
and the other said, "I got sales". And it turned out he'd

00:26:28.819 --> 00:26:31.639
never really sold anything before, but he was really, really

00:26:31.659 --> 00:26:32.659
good at it.

00:26:32.659 --> 00:26:36.399
And for the first two years they just did it, right? They

00:26:36.419 --> 00:26:42.359
did the first 10 sales by himself. So, you want to be

00:26:42.379 --> 00:26:44.069
focused enough so that you develop the

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:47.839
[inaudible] and hire around it. But you've just got to get

00:26:47.859 --> 00:26:52.500
in and do it, you know, and get stuff done. And these guys

00:26:52.519 --> 00:26:54.769
have done an exceptional job at what they're doing.

00:26:54.769 --> 00:26:59.369
Yeah. So, now I'm going to go back to the capital part, and

00:26:59.389 --> 00:27:02.389
close the loop, and say a few nice things about VC's because

00:27:02.389 --> 00:27:05.389
I started on the negative side. What we did is we actually

00:27:05.389 --> 00:27:07.659
found that, "Hey, this introduction from Jason resulted in

00:27:07.679 --> 00:27:10.579
an executive hire. We need more help hiring. We need more

00:27:10.599 --> 00:27:11.599
help understanding the business."

00:27:11.599 --> 00:27:14.279
And so, we started developing relationships with a lot of

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:16.909
different venture capitalists in Silicon Valley. I would say

00:27:16.929 --> 00:27:20.519
that venture capitalists are kind of like the nodes of

00:27:20.539 --> 00:27:23.490
activity that are happening in a lot of the different

00:27:23.509 --> 00:27:27.429
industries and companies. They have amazing visibility into,

00:27:27.449 --> 00:27:29.889
"What are the decisions getting made at the leadership

00:27:29.909 --> 00:27:31.949
level?", "What are the board meetings looking like?", and so

00:27:31.949 --> 00:27:32.949
forth.

00:27:32.949 --> 00:27:36.809
And so, as an entrepreneur you can consume a lot of

00:27:36.829 --> 00:27:40.319
information that's really valuable all entirely for free

00:27:40.339 --> 00:27:43.789
over lunches, coffees, drinks, whatever, with a wide variety

00:27:43.809 --> 00:27:46.919
of folks. And most venture capitalists when approached will

00:27:46.939 --> 00:27:48.940
definitely sit down and meet with you.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.629
And so, the key thing on developing relationships on that

00:27:52.649 --> 00:27:57.339
front that I learned was make sure that you are receptive to

00:27:57.359 --> 00:28:00.980
advice and you're going with kind of not a full cup. You're

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:04.970
making sure that the cup is half full or 3/4 full so that

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:08.359
they can give you some frameworks, some tips, and so forth.

00:28:08.379 --> 00:28:11.970
And then don't come back to them until you've implemented to

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.659
some degree what they told you to implement. I think it's

00:28:14.659 --> 00:28:17.720
very easy to say, "Oh yeah. Yeah, that's a great idea, but

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:20.470
it's not what we're doing" or something like that. And it's

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:22.649
disrespectful to then ask for help again, and ask for help

00:28:22.649 --> 00:28:24.829
again if you're not actually implementing.

00:28:24.849 --> 00:28:28.720
And so, what I try to do is just try to keep track of what

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:31.990
people are advising us to do and you know, if it makes sense

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:35.240
for us to try and so forth, you know, we'll do it. "Hey,

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.500
here's what we did." And it's a formula that worked really

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:38.849
well for us.

00:28:38.869 --> 00:28:42.950
So, after awhile I had gotten to understand more of the BC

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:47.000
process. Still, you know, 100 hour weeks into the business

00:28:47.019 --> 00:28:49.750
and just putting the sweat equity in and bootstrapping it.

00:28:49.769 --> 00:28:53.829
And then, we decided that we needed for the business reason

00:28:53.849 --> 00:28:56.849
to be better capitalized. And a lot of what drove that

00:28:56.869 --> 00:28:59.960
decision was our ambition. We were seeing success in the

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.689
marketplace, but we had multiple verticals and therefore we

00:29:02.709 --> 00:29:05.309
had multiple competitors. And we didn't want to have too

00:29:05.309 --> 00:29:07.309
small of a balance sheet to go after.

00:29:07.309 --> 00:29:11.849
And so, we decided to go through a formal process of picking

00:29:11.869 --> 00:29:16.869
seven or eight VC's that we enjoyed meeting, that we knew,

00:29:16.889 --> 00:29:19.909
and that we had some degree of a relationship with and we

00:29:19.929 --> 00:29:23.179
went through the process. I don't know if you want me to go

00:29:23.179 --> 00:29:26.119
through the nuances of that, but what ultimately resulted

00:29:26.139 --> 00:29:32.049
was choosing among a few term sheets Greg and Josh to be our

00:29:32.069 --> 00:29:33.919
partners and raise that capital.

00:29:33.939 --> 00:29:36.379
And by that time there was a long - term relationship and

00:29:36.379 --> 00:29:39.269
they met the tasks of know, like and trust. They understood

00:29:39.289 --> 00:29:43.619
our business and we were able to do a deal that we felt was

00:29:43.639 --> 00:29:44.889
win - win on both sides.

00:29:44.889 --> 00:29:47.889
I mean, I think the thing that's symmetrical in that is that

00:29:47.889 --> 00:29:49.889
longitudinal data really helps about people. So, they've

00:29:49.889 --> 00:29:53.139
gotten to know us over a period of time. I think, you know,

00:29:53.139 --> 00:29:56.279
you actually know the metrics guys for longer, right? But

00:29:56.299 --> 00:29:57.539
that does really help.

00:29:57.559 --> 00:30:01.259
And so, in the same way I think just developing those

00:30:01.279 --> 00:30:04.429
relationships will give you confidence that you're working

00:30:04.449 --> 00:30:07.909
with high quality people. But it will also give other people

00:30:07.929 --> 00:30:11.149
confidence as they say, "Oh, well we talked about this last

00:30:11.169 --> 00:30:14.419
time." So, the way I think about it is, "You told me you

00:30:14.419 --> 00:30:18.169
were going to do x. Now you came back, did you do x or do

00:30:18.169 --> 00:30:21.419
you have a good reason why you decided x wasn't the thing to

00:30:21.419 --> 00:30:22.419
do, y was?" Right?

00:30:22.419 --> 00:30:26.689
The one nuance I'll put on Matthew's point is I just always

00:30:26.709 --> 00:30:31.049
want emerging CEO's to make the decision. This is not the

00:30:31.069 --> 00:30:34.359
case necessarily with all board members or all venture

00:30:34.379 --> 00:30:38.149
firms. I'm perfectly happy to be an idea machine, but the

00:30:38.169 --> 00:30:41.429
last thing I want you to do is to go implement everything

00:30:41.449 --> 00:30:44.789
that I say because that's a recipe for disaster, right?

00:30:44.809 --> 00:30:47.720
I mean, ultimately the guy working 100 hour weeks knows more

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:50.389
about the business than anybody else in the world, right?

00:30:50.409 --> 00:30:53.899
And has got to take all that data and meet at the place

00:30:53.919 --> 00:30:56.659
where we can synthesize, and things are prioritized, and

00:30:56.679 --> 00:31:00.109
makes the call about what to do and what not to do. And I'd

00:31:00.109 --> 00:31:02.359
much rather have that conversation about, "What are the

00:31:02.359 --> 00:31:05.109
three things you're going to do?" than "What are the 10

00:31:05.109 --> 00:31:06.609
things you're not going to do?"

00:31:06.609 --> 00:31:09.609
And they can change from time to time cause I assume that

00:31:09.609 --> 00:31:11.859
you're out there learning and getting smarter every day.

00:31:11.859 --> 00:31:15.970
And the things that we do enjoy about Greg and Josh, and I

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:18.779
continue to have a philosophy around is that venture

00:31:18.799 --> 00:31:21.319
capitalists who have been in your shoes are better than

00:31:21.339 --> 00:31:22.339
[inaudible] who haven't.

00:31:22.339 --> 00:31:26.500
And so, people who have been in operating roles at companies

00:31:26.519 --> 00:31:31.029
or have started companies, they understand and speak a

00:31:31.049 --> 00:31:33.599
language that's easier for an entrepreneur to get really

00:31:33.619 --> 00:31:37.799
insightful advice about the operations of the business on.

00:31:37.819 --> 00:31:38.939
And that's been extremely helpful.

00:31:38.939 --> 00:31:42.549
And the other piece that's been extremely helpful to have

00:31:42.569 --> 00:31:45.199
these guys on is to continue to get introductions to people

00:31:45.199 --> 00:31:48.199
and scale the team. So, when you think about the value add

00:31:48.199 --> 00:31:50.740
of venture capitalists, it's expensive money in a

00:31:50.759 --> 00:31:53.659
traditional sense. You want to mainly do it when it's the

00:31:53.659 --> 00:31:56.949
right time for the business, but they will introduce you to

00:31:56.969 --> 00:32:00.220
a lot of people or have those connections available if you

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:02.789
want to take advantage of those introductions. In our case

00:32:02.809 --> 00:32:05.809
we did and that's been a huge source of referrals in terms

00:32:05.809 --> 00:32:09.389
of people joining our company.

00:32:09.409 --> 00:32:12.250
My philosophy is that basically there are four rules of

00:32:12.269 --> 00:32:15.139
venture capital once you're an existing investor. The first

00:32:15.159 --> 00:32:19.099
is physician do no harm, right? Don't try to run the

00:32:19.119 --> 00:32:21.639
company. Don't medal where you don't have information. Make

00:32:21.639 --> 00:32:24.179
sure that the CEO is running the company. The ultimate

00:32:24.199 --> 00:32:27.720
responsibility of the board is hiring and firing CEO's.

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:30.549
And then there are three places where I think you can be

00:32:30.549 --> 00:32:34.569
generally useful. One of which is strategic guidance in

00:32:34.589 --> 00:32:38.269
pattern matching. I mean, I'm sitting on 10 boards, I've

00:32:38.289 --> 00:32:42.369
probably sat on another 10 or 12. Over the history of time

00:32:42.389 --> 00:32:46.299
you just see a lot. It is a job where you're a bit, you

00:32:46.319 --> 00:32:49.730
know, a mile wide and an inch deep. You know, if you're

00:32:49.750 --> 00:32:53.000
really good you're two inches deep rather than one. But I

00:32:53.019 --> 00:32:55.409
think that does have something to do with pattern matching.

00:32:55.409 --> 00:32:59.339
And the second is you know, you're a business development

00:32:59.359 --> 00:33:02.689
arm of the company. And the third is you're a recruiting arm

00:33:02.709 --> 00:33:05.709
of the company. And those are the three places where you can

00:33:05.709 --> 00:33:08.709
really help. So, the four rules are don't' hurt, and help in

00:33:08.709 --> 00:33:11.409
those three places and you're doing your job.

00:33:11.429 --> 00:33:14.589
So, I want to flip over to Terry for a second because I

00:33:14.589 --> 00:33:18.369
think this is a bit of , you know, a different story. Right?

00:33:18.389 --> 00:33:21.139
On some level you'd say, "Well Terry is, you know, a

00:33:21.139 --> 00:33:25.710
professional CEO - had been a CEO before, had been a COO

00:33:25.730 --> 00:33:33.569
before that, you know, in very successful company." But I

00:33:33.589 --> 00:33:38.279
think also came in and told the story that just because a

00:33:38.299 --> 00:33:40.689
presentation is just telling a story, right? You're telling

00:33:40.689 --> 00:33:42.439
it with 20 PowerPoint slides, but you're just telling a

00:33:42.439 --> 00:33:43.439
story.

00:33:43.439 --> 00:33:48.509
And so, this happened for me, I don't know. Probably 15, 16,

00:33:48.529 --> 00:33:54.579
18 months ago and it just all clicked. You know, so 45

00:33:54.599 --> 00:33:57.529
minutes of presentation and all those pieces work. Now, we

00:33:57.549 --> 00:34:01.929
just want Terry to raise a very successful follow - up of

00:34:01.949 --> 00:34:06.819
venture capital with a friend and regular co - investor of

00:34:06.839 --> 00:34:12.819
mine. And another good guy who, you know, again, people

00:34:12.839 --> 00:34:17.000
matter, right? My co - investors, they matter a lot, right?

00:34:17.019 --> 00:34:21.599
But I think it'd be interesting for Terry to talk a bit

00:34:21.619 --> 00:34:26.170
about how we frame that. We actually loaded up his

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:31.029
presentation on the computer here not for him to flip

00:34:31.049 --> 00:34:33.799
through the whole thing, but I think there are some key

00:34:33.799 --> 00:34:38.009
slides that are, you know, potentially good examples to say,

00:34:38.029 --> 00:34:41.789
you know, "Your company's different, but you have to have

00:34:41.809 --> 00:34:44.059
one that looks like this to solve this problem."

00:34:44.059 --> 00:34:46.809
So, I don't know if you want to talk a little bit more about

00:34:46.809 --> 00:34:47.809
[overlapping] first.

00:34:47.809 --> 00:34:52.909
Let me give you a little context on the company and what

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:55.429
stages it went through when Greg invested. I'd probably add

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:58.679
a fifth to your list of things to help with and that is

00:34:58.679 --> 00:35:01.529
fundraising in the next round, targeting, focusing, finding

00:35:01.549 --> 00:35:07.489
the right fit because there are a lot of VC's out there. You

00:35:07.509 --> 00:35:10.659
know, I'm not an expert on what VC's are and what they focus

00:35:10.659 --> 00:35:13.909
on. But Greg is and was able to focus on really getting us

00:35:13.909 --> 00:35:15.159
very focused in this round.

00:35:15.159 --> 00:35:20.569
We raised $27 million to date. We just raised $11 million of

00:35:20.589 --> 00:35:23.349
it. We announced it this week. We raised it a couple of

00:35:23.349 --> 00:35:27.559
weeks ago. That's essentially our series "C". Greg invested

00:35:27.579 --> 00:35:32.449
essentially our series "B" which is a harder round to invest

00:35:32.469 --> 00:35:37.500
in because you kind of have to have a shared vision with the

00:35:37.519 --> 00:35:41.179
investor about where the market's going. The way I

00:35:41.199 --> 00:35:44.670
characterize it is in some ways "A" is easier because it's

00:35:44.690 --> 00:35:47.690
all promise. If you believe the idea's big, and the market's

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:50.839
big, and the people are smart it's all promise. You don't

00:35:50.839 --> 00:35:53.329
have any reality to bet on yet. So, you're betting on the

00:35:53.329 --> 00:35:54.329
dream.

00:35:54.329 --> 00:35:59.079
"B" has some reality and it's not all good. You know, the

00:35:59.099 --> 00:36:02.179
company had a product that solved a problem. We had six or

00:36:02.199 --> 00:36:06.110
seven customers, but hadn't added customers very quickly. We

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:11.590
didn't have a lot of repeatability. You know, so Greg had to

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:17.259
believe in enough of the vision of what we could do with the

00:36:17.279 --> 00:36:20.400
proof points that we had, and had to believe in the team,

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:23.569
and you know, that we'd be able to execute on the

00:36:23.589 --> 00:36:26.710
fundamental things that we needed to change to make business

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:30.130
really, really turn, and sort of have the patience to see

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:31.319
that through with you.

00:36:31.339 --> 00:36:34.089
So you know, we found that with Greg and Sutter Hill.

00:36:34.089 --> 00:36:35.589
And actually, can I make a...

00:36:34.839 --> 00:36:35.839
Yeah.

00:36:35.839 --> 00:36:39.319
couple of additional observations right now cause this is,

00:36:39.339 --> 00:36:42.190
again sort of my framework as an outsider coming into the

00:36:42.210 --> 00:36:44.710
business and what made that work because there's one other

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:47.460
thing about the company, right? Which is it went through its

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:52.829
series "A", but over two and a half or three years, I mean,

00:36:52.849 --> 00:36:55.400
all with the foundational capital which is good for the

00:36:55.420 --> 00:36:57.029
investor, right?

00:36:57.049 --> 00:37:00.339
But God, you know, two and a half or three years, and $8

00:37:00.359 --> 00:37:03.199
million, or $9 million, or $10 million is like it spent some

00:37:03.199 --> 00:37:08.409
time looking for its future, trying to get chronic market

00:37:08.429 --> 00:37:16.319
fit. You know, me I'm always trying to say, "When you walk

00:37:16.339 --> 00:37:19.799
in the door do you have product market fit" is the

00:37:19.819 --> 00:37:25.250
fundamental question and not, "Has it been a completely

00:37:25.269 --> 00:37:28.500
linear path?" There are a lot of people who wouldn't want to

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:31.250
look at something that has been around for three years and

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:34.000
is just getting there. That doesn't bother me at all, right?

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.279
But then to this same point you say, "Well, what are the

00:37:37.299 --> 00:37:40.909
access points that made it easy to make a couple of those

00:37:40.929 --> 00:37:47.799
bets?" So, the first is Terry, right? So, the fact that the

00:37:47.819 --> 00:37:51.949
story was so clear, and crisp, and well articulated, and

00:37:51.969 --> 00:37:55.190
that when you periodically drilled down into something he

00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:57.710
had good, thoughtful answers. Even if I didn't agree with

00:37:57.710 --> 00:37:59.960
them necessarily they were thoughtful and well thought out.

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:01.569
There was a rationale behind them.

00:38:01.589 --> 00:38:07.299
And you know, that really matters. The place where it's

00:38:07.319 --> 00:38:11.529
hardest to do that honesty is in the financial model because

00:38:11.549 --> 00:38:15.329
it's all assumptions, right? But even the notion that, "Hey,

00:38:15.349 --> 00:38:17.849
you've been cleared by who your customer segment is, which

00:38:17.849 --> 00:38:20.880
features they need, how you're pricing, what's tapped

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:24.319
market, and that it's all lined up and strategically

00:38:24.339 --> 00:38:27.319
consistent", right? Basic stuff, but a lot of times it

00:38:27.339 --> 00:38:28.339
doesn't show up.

00:38:28.339 --> 00:38:31.929
But you know, the other thing about Terry is we have a very

00:38:31.949 --> 00:38:35.369
close, mutual friend, right? A good personal friend of mine

00:38:35.389 --> 00:38:38.659
who had worked closely with Terry in the last company. And

00:38:38.679 --> 00:38:42.549
I'm telling you, it's an example of, you know, the weak

00:38:42.569 --> 00:38:46.989
ties. It makes a difference because I'd call one guy and

00:38:47.009 --> 00:38:51.559
say, "Just tell me the truth", right? And so, that makes a

00:38:51.579 --> 00:38:52.579
big difference.

00:38:52.579 --> 00:38:56.900
The second is this whole predicted analytics technology step

00:38:56.920 --> 00:39:01.049
which nobody else is using in banking fraud prevention, but

00:39:01.069 --> 00:39:05.159
a lot of people are using in internet monetization, and

00:39:05.179 --> 00:39:07.619
[inaudible], you know, and the like. So, I was very familiar

00:39:07.619 --> 00:39:11.110
with the technologies that even though it was being applied

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:14.589
in a totally different place and I had experts that I could

00:39:14.609 --> 00:39:17.609
bring to say, you know, "Can you help me come kick the

00:39:17.609 --> 00:39:20.170
tires?" And if it's a completely different technology staff

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:23.190
where I don't know the experts it's just that much harder to

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:24.190
get my arms around.

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:28.819
And the third is the target customer segment. My sister runs

00:39:28.839 --> 00:39:32.610
one of these banks, right? And so, I mean I grew up knowing,

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:35.269
you know, for most people you say, "I'm selling to community

00:39:35.269 --> 00:39:38.139
banks" and you're like, "Well, I do business with Wells

00:39:38.159 --> 00:39:42.690
Fargo or B of A". I don't know any of those, right? I'm not

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:44.839
used to it. Venture bank companies aren't usually selling to

00:39:44.839 --> 00:39:45.839
them.

00:39:45.839 --> 00:39:49.170
So again, I come back to this notion of these little access

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:53.009
points demystified. So, when you're looking for, you know,

00:39:53.029 --> 00:39:55.159
an investor it sometimes is worthwhile...

00:39:55.179 --> 00:40:00.299
From the entrepreneur side going through that, and we talked

00:40:00.319 --> 00:40:03.980
with a few firms to raise series "B". And series "B" was

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:07.469
hard to raise because we had been around for a few years and

00:40:07.489 --> 00:40:11.940
you know, we had some reality but not a ton of proof on

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:15.000
where we were trying to take the company. And you know,

00:40:15.019 --> 00:40:18.469
there were some VC's who went through really deep diligence

00:40:18.489 --> 00:40:23.619
with us - painful, excruciating diligence. I didn't

00:40:23.639 --> 00:40:26.739
necessarily feel like I had a lot of choices at the time,

00:40:26.759 --> 00:40:30.509
but if I did feel like I had a lot of choices the fact that

00:40:30.509 --> 00:40:32.509
they were taking me through such excruciating detailed

00:40:32.509 --> 00:40:37.049
diligence would have been a sign that we were probably not

00:40:37.069 --> 00:40:38.069
aligned.

00:40:38.069 --> 00:40:40.319
Great due diligence. It wasn't painful. He asked for

00:40:40.319 --> 00:40:43.319
information, but he had a lot of touch points already and he

00:40:43.319 --> 00:40:46.839
had a lot of confidence in the technology stack, and the

00:40:46.859 --> 00:40:49.859
market, and the viability of it. And he wanted to vet the

00:40:49.859 --> 00:40:52.859
things that he should vet. So, you didn't feel like you were

00:40:52.859 --> 00:40:56.309
re - explaining the rationale behind the business every time

00:40:56.329 --> 00:40:59.079
you interacted with him. In some cases, you know, that's a

00:40:59.079 --> 00:41:01.630
sign. If you keep explaining the same things to your

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:04.599
potential investor and they keep asking the same questions

00:41:04.619 --> 00:41:07.460
over and over that's not alignment. You know, that's not

00:41:07.460 --> 00:41:10.469
good alignment. They may think it's a great idea, and want

00:41:10.489 --> 00:41:14.230
to invest, and want to believe, but you really need - I

00:41:14.250 --> 00:41:17.319
think everybody's said - the cultural fit, chemistry and

00:41:17.339 --> 00:41:20.869
alignment are probably the most important thing in getting

00:41:20.889 --> 00:41:23.130
this to work because you don't work together through thick

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:24.130
and thin.

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:28.230
And your business plan is not going to be what happens. Your

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:33.119
business plan is a spreadsheet and a set of projections

00:41:33.139 --> 00:41:36.559
based on your best information right now. And things are

00:41:36.579 --> 00:41:39.579
going to change. You have to know why. You have to be

00:41:39.589 --> 00:41:42.089
thoughtful about why and you have to be thoughtful about

00:41:42.089 --> 00:41:45.339
what to do next when things do change. And you know, you can

00:41:45.339 --> 00:41:48.589
come in under your plan, you can come in over your plan, but

00:41:48.589 --> 00:41:51.589
I guarantee you you're not going to come in exactly at the

00:41:51.589 --> 00:41:54.339
plan you put down when you go talk to an investor about

00:41:54.339 --> 00:41:55.339
funding the business.

00:41:55.339 --> 00:42:02.339
And we've done both. We've done both together and when you

00:42:02.359 --> 00:42:05.980
know you have alignment is when, you know, the quarterly

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:08.559
results didn't come out the way you expected and you're

00:42:08.559 --> 00:42:12.739
lower. And the reaction is, "Well, let's understand the

00:42:12.759 --> 00:42:15.619
fundamentals. What's going on? What's your assessment of

00:42:15.639 --> 00:42:19.529
why? And are the basic premise of how we're building the

00:42:19.549 --> 00:42:22.610
business are that the customer's still buying it, that the

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:25.480
market is still receptive, that your product plan is still

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:29.230
tracking, are all those still in place? Okay. Let's go

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:30.250
forward."

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:33.000
And in fact, I can remember one quarterly meeting after Greg

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:36.549
had invested where the management team was so sort of

00:42:36.569 --> 00:42:40.369
distressed at not having made their numbers that Greg and

00:42:40.389 --> 00:42:43.429
the other investors were actually being the cheerleaders in

00:42:43.449 --> 00:42:46.839
the board meeting explaining to our management team that, "

00:42:46.859 --> 00:42:50.009
Hey, everything you're doing is pushing the rock up the hill

00:42:50.029 --> 00:42:52.529
and things are going to get better because you're executing

00:42:52.529 --> 00:42:54.779
on all the fundamentals that we all believe in."

00:42:54.779 --> 00:42:58.039
So, it wasn't about this kind of maniacal focus on, "Why did

00:42:58.059 --> 00:43:00.809
you miss this number?" "Why didn't you get this deal closed?

00:43:00.809 --> 00:43:05.079
" It was on the fundamentals and the metrics of the business

00:43:05.099 --> 00:43:07.599
and that's real alignment. That's real alignment on why you'

00:43:07.599 --> 00:43:10.599
re doing it. And you know, I think it's been a great

00:43:10.599 --> 00:43:11.599
partnership from that standpoint.

00:43:11.599 --> 00:43:15.079
Series "C" is actually what we just raised is a different

00:43:15.099 --> 00:43:20.059
round. You know, we were able to - with our series "B" money

00:43:20.079 --> 00:43:23.230
- execute a lot of things that we wanted to execute. We were

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:26.000
able to transition the business to a Sass model, we were

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:28.500
able to restructure our pricing and our go to market

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:30.750
approach, we were able to develop some channel partnerships

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:34.000
that are starting to pay off, and we're able to get a level

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:37.509
of repeatability in the sales model that, you know, is still

00:43:37.529 --> 00:43:40.549
not going to be perfect and is not going to trend exactly

00:43:40.569 --> 00:43:43.319
how we think . But we're able to competently say, "Look, we'

00:43:43.319 --> 00:43:46.569
ve got a couple of quarters under our belt where we said we

00:43:46.569 --> 00:43:49.069
were going to sign this many customers, we did. We're

00:43:49.069 --> 00:43:51.569
deploying quickly, we transitioned to the Sass model and it'

00:43:51.569 --> 00:43:54.569
s working." So, we've got a lot of vectors that are working.

00:43:54.569 --> 00:43:57.319
There are still a lot of uncertainties, but it's more. You

00:43:57.319 --> 00:44:00.569
know, I think of series "C" as you've hit a pace where you'

00:44:00.569 --> 00:44:03.239
re repeatable and somewhat predictable, and you want to pour

00:44:03.259 --> 00:44:06.009
some fuel on the fire and grow on that predictability and

00:44:06.009 --> 00:44:08.839
repeatability.

00:44:08.859 --> 00:44:13.710
So actually, one of the things that I want to do in two

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:16.739
different ways with these two different companies is

00:44:16.759 --> 00:44:21.579
actually pull on this thread of what I'll call transitioning

00:44:21.599 --> 00:44:25.089
the business and you know, when you decide to do it, how you

00:44:25.109 --> 00:44:28.609
do it, and what the risks are of doing it. And I think in

00:44:28.609 --> 00:44:30.579
the Inflection case it's launching new product categories,

00:44:30.579 --> 00:44:31.579
right?

00:44:31.579 --> 00:44:36.659
And so, I think one of the guys who I have tremendous

00:44:36.679 --> 00:44:39.859
respect for is Ben Horowitz who's Mark Andreessen's partner

00:44:39.879 --> 00:44:43.259
at Andreesen-Horowitz. Ben and I were product managers

00:44:43.279 --> 00:44:48.639
together at Netscape earlier in my career. And he's one of

00:44:48.659 --> 00:44:51.909
the guys that I look at and say, "Oh, he's doing the same

00:44:51.909 --> 00:44:56.839
job I am, but he's just much better at it."

00:44:56.859 --> 00:45:03.099
But you know to me, interestingly, I would say, you know,

00:45:03.119 --> 00:45:05.869
that's how you learn, right? I mean, babies learn because

00:45:05.869 --> 00:45:08.279
they mimic the adults and the older kids in their

00:45:08.279 --> 00:45:12.079
environment. And I think, you know, the life lesson is you

00:45:12.099 --> 00:45:14.839
surround yourself with great people, and you learn what

00:45:14.859 --> 00:45:17.579
they're doing, and you mimic it. And I have this theory

00:45:17.579 --> 00:45:20.329
coming out of business school that I had just learned by

00:45:20.329 --> 00:45:23.269
running my own experiments and learning faster. Turns out

00:45:23.289 --> 00:45:26.779
that's a much slower way to learn and it's a lot easier just

00:45:26.779 --> 00:45:29.529
to go to great companies with great people and learn from

00:45:29.529 --> 00:45:31.869
what they do. You'll make plenty of your own mistakes along

00:45:31.869 --> 00:45:32.869
the way.

00:45:32.869 --> 00:45:36.239
But you know, one of the things Ben said, "when he was

00:45:36.259 --> 00:45:39.420
running Opsware was basically launching a new product -

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:43.549
version 1.0 of a product is the single hardest thing you do.

00:45:43.569 --> 00:45:45.940
" You know, I have many product managers or engineering

00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:49.210
leads who can do version 2, and 3, and 4. When there's a

00:45:49.210 --> 00:45:52.210
bunch of data and customers to talk to version 1.0 is

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:55.400
brutal. And there are only four or five people in the

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:59.089
company who I trust to do that, who got enough intuition

00:45:59.109 --> 00:46:02.219
about product/market fit. And if I don't have one of those

00:46:02.219 --> 00:46:04.969
people available I won't let a project start. Either I'm not

00:46:04.969 --> 00:46:06.969
going to do it or I'll buy it.

00:46:06.969 --> 00:46:10.219
And so I think, you know, if you maybe want to talk about

00:46:10.219 --> 00:46:12.969
launching, you know, any new products cause I think that is

00:46:12.969 --> 00:46:16.849
part of what opened up the business, opened up the available

00:46:16.869 --> 00:46:19.559
market for the company and put it on a bigger stage.

00:46:19.559 --> 00:46:23.119
Yeah. How many people here are interested in launching a

00:46:23.139 --> 00:46:26.359
consumer facing type of product? Just so I can get a sense

00:46:26.379 --> 00:46:27.379
of consumer internet.

00:46:27.379 --> 00:46:28.379
[inaudible]. B2B? No? Okay.

00:46:28.379 --> 00:46:40.279
So for us, I mean launching a product's really tough. The #1

00:46:40.299 --> 00:46:45.569
set of lessons I got was by doing it to feed myself. When I

00:46:45.589 --> 00:46:49.809
was in college I had no money. I was working for $6 an hour

00:46:49.829 --> 00:46:52.739
at some work study program, was going to school and I was

00:46:52.739 --> 00:46:56.769
trying to figure out how to sell eBooks to high school

00:46:56.789 --> 00:46:59.929
students on how to get into college. And so, I was writing

00:46:59.949 --> 00:47:03.440
sales letters, and creating marketing material, and trying

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:06.779
to convince people to do it, and do it over the phone. And

00:47:06.799 --> 00:47:08.799
that actually was the best single learning experience

00:47:08.799 --> 00:47:13.559
because when you're at that stage you quickly get the

00:47:13.579 --> 00:47:16.569
lessons. It's much better than reading it out of some book.

00:47:16.569 --> 00:47:21.420
But you know, taking it up a level I think that you want to

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:24.349
be data driven, you want to be quantitative. Greg talked

00:47:24.369 --> 00:47:27.400
about that. It's one of our core values at our company. We'

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:30.239
re constantly looking for folks who are analytical and who

00:47:30.259 --> 00:47:33.529
are thinking. We call it not intellectual honesty. We call

00:47:33.549 --> 00:47:38.019
it thinking clearly reflection. "Are they a clear thinker?"

00:47:38.039 --> 00:47:41.759
is one of our benchmark kind of questions on interviews.

00:47:41.779 --> 00:47:45.429
And so, you want to be listening to the data because we all

00:47:45.449 --> 00:47:49.049
have ideas. Ideas are a dime a dozen and we all can have

00:47:49.069 --> 00:47:52.069
intuition about what's going to work but at the end of the

00:47:52.069 --> 00:47:55.589
day, users will surprise you no matter what. And what we've

00:47:55.609 --> 00:48:00.000
created, you know, it's still continuing to morph and form

00:48:00.019 --> 00:48:03.389
at Inflection. But we've created essentially a loop where

00:48:03.409 --> 00:48:07.119
kind of a product manager, someone who's the quarterback of

00:48:07.139 --> 00:48:10.769
the product you're going to launch or the feature. It could

00:48:10.789 --> 00:48:13.679
be either. And then you have Engineering who's going to

00:48:13.699 --> 00:48:16.609
build it, you have Design who's going to design it and

00:48:16.609 --> 00:48:19.079
preferably the wire frames, mockups, everything else. And

00:48:19.099 --> 00:48:21.849
then you have an analyst who is going to continue to

00:48:21.849 --> 00:48:24.129
research the data, understand what it's saying and report

00:48:24.129 --> 00:48:25.129
back.

00:48:25.129 --> 00:48:29.460
And that feedback loop with some QA involved and a few other

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:34.589
functions is really kind of the magic formula for us. We've

00:48:34.609 --> 00:48:37.750
launched a ton of websites, and features, and products at

00:48:37.769 --> 00:48:41.589
Inflection. And what we've tried to do is along the way look

00:48:41.609 --> 00:48:45.159
at the patterns for us of what are emerging and then put

00:48:45.179 --> 00:48:48.199
those into the platform so that we can continue to be faster

00:48:48.219 --> 00:48:49.969
at launching things.

00:48:49.989 --> 00:48:53.279
I will say that the most successful launches we've done have

00:48:53.299 --> 00:48:57.929
always been, you know, the three weeks before that no one

00:48:57.949 --> 00:49:01.839
slept hardly at all. Everyone was up all hours of the night.

00:49:01.859 --> 00:49:05.029
There are always millions of more details to make these

00:49:05.049 --> 00:49:08.969
things work than you ever expect going in. There is no

00:49:08.989 --> 00:49:11.589
replacement for just hard work, rolling up your sleeves and

00:49:11.609 --> 00:49:14.609
getting it done. And I think that you need to be working

00:49:14.609 --> 00:49:17.109
with people that, you know, you can go to war with in that

00:49:17.109 --> 00:49:18.109
regard.

00:49:18.109 --> 00:49:22.639
I remember, you know, myself and one engineer being at the

00:49:22.659 --> 00:49:25.869
office at all hours of the night one time when we were

00:49:25.889 --> 00:49:28.389
launching a website and just looking over and thinking, "If

00:49:28.389 --> 00:49:32.279
I just didn't inherently trust this person, if we weren't

00:49:32.299 --> 00:49:35.179
totally on the same page this would have never worked. We

00:49:35.199 --> 00:49:38.809
could have never gotten this far." And companies are so

00:49:38.829 --> 00:49:41.559
bureaucratic once they start growing just by nature of

00:49:41.579 --> 00:49:45.079
adding a lot of people to the company that I definitely

00:49:45.099 --> 00:49:47.989
would never underestimate the value of, you know, whoever

00:49:48.009 --> 00:49:50.259
you're working on with that initial product launch. You've

00:49:50.259 --> 00:49:53.259
just got to make sure you guys are on the same page and the

00:49:53.259 --> 00:49:54.259
same wavelength.

00:49:54.259 --> 00:49:59.299
So Archives.com, you know, basically has become, you know,

00:49:59.319 --> 00:50:03.250
half the business or something, right? And you know, it's a

00:50:03.269 --> 00:50:06.019
two year old product, right? So, it was launched two years

00:50:06.019 --> 00:50:09.849
into history. Was it, you know, one more website among many

00:50:09.869 --> 00:50:16.809
that you'd launched when you guys decided to do it? Or did

00:50:16.829 --> 00:50:20.289
you know it was a big deal and therefore it was launched

00:50:20.309 --> 00:50:21.309
with more forethought?

00:50:21.309 --> 00:50:26.069
Yeah, it's a combination of all things, but you know, just

00:50:26.089 --> 00:50:28.460
the metaphors we use - throw the spaghetti against the wall

00:50:28.460 --> 00:50:32.139
and see if it sticks. We use an agile development cycle. So,

00:50:32.159 --> 00:50:34.909
oftentimes we're throwing things against the wall and if

00:50:34.929 --> 00:50:37.679
they start gaining traction we add more resources to it. If

00:50:37.699 --> 00:50:40.799
they really look exciting or promising then we really dig

00:50:40.819 --> 00:50:43.759
in. We may throw away everything that we've already done to

00:50:43.759 --> 00:50:46.309
date because we think it's such an opportunity that we need

00:50:46.309 --> 00:50:48.809
to build it, you know, in a more substantial way.

00:50:48.809 --> 00:50:52.230
But one of the things that an entrepreneur friend of mine

00:50:52.250 --> 00:50:55.059
told me that's really stuck with me is don't weigh

00:50:55.059 --> 00:50:58.759
opportunities equally. You'll see so many opportunities in

00:50:58.779 --> 00:51:01.639
your business for improvement it's easy to lose sight of the

00:51:01.639 --> 00:51:04.349
fact that, you know, some opportunities are, you know, 50

00:51:04.369 --> 00:51:07.179
times more important than others. You know, hire a really

00:51:07.199 --> 00:51:11.420
phenomenal VP of Engineering vs. you know, fixing the code

00:51:11.440 --> 00:51:15.299
on that one site or optimizing the checkout process,

00:51:15.319 --> 00:51:17.569
something like that. They just can't be weighted equally.

00:51:17.569 --> 00:51:20.649
They can't make it to your to do list equally. They can't

00:51:20.649 --> 00:51:21.649
consume your mindshare equally.

00:51:21.649 --> 00:51:25.699
So, for us, we saw that, "Hey, there's this baby boomer

00:51:25.719 --> 00:51:29.589
demographic shift that's happening in the United States. We'

00:51:29.609 --> 00:51:32.389
re acquiring all this historical data and it doesn't look

00:51:32.409 --> 00:51:36.159
like a lot of people are actually acquiring and organizing -

00:51:36.179 --> 00:51:39.819
and everything from census records, to newspapers, to

00:51:39.839 --> 00:51:43.839
obituaries, to death records internationally. And we see

00:51:43.859 --> 00:51:46.789
that people online are very interested in genealogy.

00:51:46.809 --> 00:51:49.929
Meanwhile, the leading company in the space - Ancestry.com -

00:51:49.949 --> 00:51:54.029
has a product that's over $200 a year which doesn't really

00:51:54.049 --> 00:51:57.440
appeal to a beginning person who's interested in their

00:51:57.460 --> 00:51:59.009
family history.

00:51:59.029 --> 00:52:03.440
Their S1 provided the data including the fact that 15% of

00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:07.079
folks in the United States express an interest in family

00:52:07.099 --> 00:52:09.679
history. That's kind of interesting. And we started putting

00:52:09.699 --> 00:52:11.719
all these pieces together and said, "You know what? This

00:52:11.719 --> 00:52:12.719
makes sense."

00:52:12.719 --> 00:52:15.179
So, the first site we launched was called GeneologyArchives.

00:52:15.199 --> 00:52:19.259
com and it was doing amazingly well right from the get go,

00:52:19.279 --> 00:52:23.089
and it had a lot of head room. So, we went out and found the

00:52:23.109 --> 00:52:25.649
person that owned Archives.com. They weren't using it. We

00:52:25.649 --> 00:52:29.389
made a big bet because we didn't have easy money and that

00:52:29.409 --> 00:52:32.349
was an expensive acquisition. But we made a big bet from

00:52:32.369 --> 00:52:35.349
retaining the earnings, put the bet on the table and we're

00:52:35.349 --> 00:52:38.730
now the #2 family history website. Our product's $40 a year

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:42.539
for unlimited access. We're growing very rapidly in that

00:52:42.559 --> 00:52:46.159
category and it's got a lot of growing room ahead. So, I

00:52:46.179 --> 00:52:49.099
think that's kind of a story of how we did it.

00:52:49.099 --> 00:52:52.440
You know, I think from there I would just love to maybe ask

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:55.519
Q &amp; A when we get to Q &amp; A of what other nuances we can go

00:52:55.519 --> 00:52:56.519
into.

00:52:56.519 --> 00:52:59.019
Yeah. I mean, the thing I think is interesting about

00:52:59.019 --> 00:53:01.269
consumer nano products is there's so much feedback and

00:53:01.269 --> 00:53:04.949
there's so much data. So you know, product management - the

00:53:04.969 --> 00:53:07.879
product market big piece is always some part analytical and

00:53:07.899 --> 00:53:12.829
some part creative. And the creative part is, "What do you

00:53:12.849 --> 00:53:14.349
put out there to test?" Right?

00:53:14.349 --> 00:53:19.389
So, if you don't have some bit of that creative instinct or

00:53:19.409 --> 00:53:23.150
judgment about what people might want, then you can put a

00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:26.170
bunch of things out and they'll all fail. If you get the

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:29.119
right kind of people to make those judgments - hopefully

00:53:29.139 --> 00:53:32.779
often the founder, right, or one of the founders - then you

00:53:32.799 --> 00:53:35.869
can start analyzing data and figure that out.

00:53:35.889 --> 00:53:40.069
You know, I think the other half of the transformation piece

00:53:40.089 --> 00:53:44.679
- and this is one of the bets I was willing to make at

00:53:44.699 --> 00:53:47.199
Guardian. I think maybe some other people weren't and that

00:53:47.199 --> 00:53:51.569
has gone exceptionally well but is really, really hard is -

00:53:51.589 --> 00:53:54.909
in service of trying to create better business is this

00:53:54.929 --> 00:54:00.139
transformation to Sass. So, from an enterprise software

00:54:00.159 --> 00:54:02.329
customer deploys in their own environment on their own

00:54:02.329 --> 00:54:03.329
hardware...

00:54:03.329 --> 00:54:04.329
Yeah.

00:54:04.329 --> 00:54:07.920
to a Sass based solution. And I wonder if you could talk a

00:54:07.940 --> 00:54:14.719
little bit about both what that was like to actually do, but

00:54:14.739 --> 00:54:18.859
also how transformative it's been to the business model.

00:54:18.879 --> 00:54:21.629
Yeah. It's been huge and you know, it wasn't a technology

00:54:21.629 --> 00:54:24.379
decision. It was a strategy decision and it was a marketing

00:54:24.379 --> 00:54:26.629
decision to begin with. And I'll give you a little of that

00:54:26.629 --> 00:54:27.629
context.

00:54:27.629 --> 00:54:31.480
The company started out like most security companies in the

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:34.670
Financial Services space saying, "We've got to get Bank of

00:54:34.690 --> 00:54:39.049
America, and Citibank, and Wells Fargo. And you know, that's

00:54:39.069 --> 00:54:42.420
going to validate the product and put us on the map." And

00:54:42.440 --> 00:54:45.690
they tried that. We tried it for a couple of years before I

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:48.440
got there, but we tried it for a couple of years.

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:51.670
I think the biggest banks we succeeded getting were Zion - a

00:54:51.690 --> 00:54:54.009
pretty good sized bank, First Republic, Silicon Valley Bank,

00:54:54.029 --> 00:54:57.769
you know, decent sized banks but not he really big guys.

00:54:57.789 --> 00:55:00.440
And then we were starting to have some success with smaller

00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:04.980
banks - regional community banks. But we were this on prem

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:10.940
model. And you know, they had to deploy it, they had to buy

00:55:10.960 --> 00:55:14.460
a server, they had to start an IT project. We had to go on

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:17.710
site, we had to stand up the system, we had to integrate to

00:55:17.710 --> 00:55:19.960
all the different databases and operating systems that big

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:25.589
IT shops have. Inevitably with the bigger banks we ran into

00:55:25.609 --> 00:55:28.889
the, "Well, let's just tweak this and customize that, fit it

00:55:28.909 --> 00:55:32.049
into our environment this way". So, every job was kind of

00:55:32.069 --> 00:55:34.969
this big, massive effort to get it working.

00:55:34.989 --> 00:55:37.619
And we looked at the market and said, "Well look, there's

00:55:37.619 --> 00:55:41.219
17,000 or so banks in the U.S. Thirty of them are really big

00:55:41.219 --> 00:55:44.000
and look like Bank of America and Wells Fargo and the other

00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:50.179
16,970 don't. And most of those guys actually buy their

00:55:50.199 --> 00:55:54.829
online banking system from one of about 10 providers. There'

00:55:54.849 --> 00:55:58.000
s Intuit, there's Buy Serv, there's Fidelity, there's Jack

00:55:58.019 --> 00:55:59.699
Henry and a handful of others.

00:55:59.719 --> 00:56:02.440
So, the market's actually consolidated around these internet

00:56:02.460 --> 00:56:07.000
banking platforms and these banks buy outsourced services

00:56:07.019 --> 00:56:10.079
because they buy their internet banking platform from a

00:56:10.099 --> 00:56:13.150
essentially Sass provider. I don't think they called it Sass

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:17.469
when they first introduced those things. And they buy

00:56:17.489 --> 00:56:20.599
solutions. They don't want to buy something and rip it apart

00:56:20.619 --> 00:56:24.559
and reconfigure it to fit into their custom environment. And

00:56:24.579 --> 00:56:27.859
they make decisions. And oh by the way, they're also

00:56:27.879 --> 00:56:31.769
completely under protected from fraud. And the fraudsters

00:56:31.789 --> 00:56:34.179
have figured it out and started attacking them. So, the

00:56:34.179 --> 00:56:36.429
problems have merely migrated from the big banks down to

00:56:36.429 --> 00:56:37.429
these small guys.

00:56:37.429 --> 00:56:40.739
So we said, "That's who we should go after. That's really

00:56:40.759 --> 00:56:43.969
who's going to buy our product." But to do it we have to

00:56:43.969 --> 00:56:47.429
have a Sass solution. For all the kind of cost and

00:56:47.449 --> 00:56:50.869
complexity issues, but also that's the thing that this

00:56:50.889 --> 00:56:54.759
market needs. So, we decided to make that transition. It was

00:56:54.779 --> 00:56:58.940
not an easy decision to make. We put a lot of thought into

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:02.460
it and we had to raise money. And we added this to that and

00:57:02.460 --> 00:57:04.960
said, "Well, we've decided this for these reasons and we

00:57:04.960 --> 00:57:07.710
know we can execute and make this transition to Sass." And

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:10.210
that's kind of a scary prospect because companies try to

00:57:10.210 --> 00:57:11.210
make a transition from

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:15.940
[inaudible] to Sass and it's not always easy.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:17.889
We were lucky in a couple of ways -

00:57:17.889 --> 00:57:20.639
We didn't have to change our business model. We charged a

00:57:20.639 --> 00:57:22.889
subscription for our software from the beginning. So, even

00:57:22.889 --> 00:57:25.889
though it was deployed on prem we didn't have to change the

00:57:25.889 --> 00:57:28.629
way people paid for it.

00:57:28.649 --> 00:57:32.409
We knew that this was going to be hard, so we anticipated

00:57:32.429 --> 00:57:35.059
it. We hired a really extraordinary

00:57:35.079 --> 00:57:38.269
[inaudible] person who I happened to know through previous

00:57:38.289 --> 00:57:43.569
companies. He worked with me at Good Technology and his boss

00:57:43.589 --> 00:57:48.869
worked with me at Good Technology, and at Market Live. So, I

00:57:48.889 --> 00:57:52.389
knew him. I knew he had a pedigree and could set it up. He

00:57:52.389 --> 00:57:55.639
could do it and he was willing to really put the effort in

00:57:55.639 --> 00:57:58.440
to do it from scratch. And we know we're dealing with

00:57:58.440 --> 00:58:02.960
personally identifiable information about account holders in

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:05.480
banks in our hosting center and we're a venture back

00:58:05.480 --> 00:58:09.359
startup. So, we knew security might come up in the

00:58:09.379 --> 00:58:10.379
conversation.

00:58:10.379 --> 00:58:17.049
So you know, we anticipated that from the get go and we went

00:58:17.069 --> 00:58:20.549
and talked to these banking platform providers and to our

00:58:20.569 --> 00:58:23.799
existing customers and said, "Give us your security audits.

00:58:23.819 --> 00:58:26.569
If we came to you with a hosted solution, what is the

00:58:26.569 --> 00:58:28.849
security audit that you would put us through?"

00:58:28.869 --> 00:58:32.119
And we got a half a dozen of these security audits and we

00:58:32.119 --> 00:58:35.159
also got an outside consulting firm that does security

00:58:35.179 --> 00:58:40.379
audits for banks. And we designed our Sass solution based on

00:58:40.399 --> 00:58:43.789
those security audits. So, we're Sass 70 level 2 compliant,

00:58:43.809 --> 00:58:48.949
all those things. We also have a dozen security audit check

00:58:48.969 --> 00:58:52.769
lists from real banks in our file drawer that we checked off

00:58:52.789 --> 00:58:55.440
as we were designing and building the Sass solution.

00:58:55.460 --> 00:58:58.619
So, when we migrated, and we've gone out, and we started

00:58:58.639 --> 00:59:01.710
selling and they said, "Well, how do you pass the security

00:59:01.730 --> 00:59:04.480
audit?" They pull out there's. Well, we've got as part of

00:59:04.480 --> 00:59:06.980
the Vin diagram we've already solved for all those things

00:59:06.980 --> 00:59:10.609
and we haven't had a single issue. We haven't not gotten a

00:59:10.629 --> 00:59:13.690
single deal because of security concerns which is one of the

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:17.730
things that kept me up at night for nine months while we

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:19.000
made the transition to Sass.

00:59:19.000 --> 00:59:22.480
So, it was very successful. And by the time we actually

00:59:22.500 --> 00:59:26.079
turned the switch on the Sass solution - it was just this

00:59:26.099 --> 00:59:30.980
past August - I think we had 17 customers on

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:34.679
[inaudible] and all but three have said definitively that

00:59:34.699 --> 00:59:38.849
they're going to migrate to the Sass solution. So, that's

00:59:38.869 --> 00:59:43.989
pretty good. And we've added 25 or so new customers to the

00:59:44.009 --> 00:59:45.049
Sass solution.

00:59:45.069 --> 00:59:48.349
So, talk about things like, you know, deploying timeframes

00:59:48.369 --> 00:59:49.369
to value...

00:59:49.369 --> 00:59:50.369
Yeah.

00:59:50.369 --> 00:59:53.210
sales cycles and the like cause I think this is part of the

00:59:53.210 --> 00:59:54.710
benefit of the Sass business model...

00:59:53.960 --> 00:59:54.960
Yeah.

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:57.879
that has just been proven now today.

00:59:57.899 --> 01:00:02.460
Well, it's massive. You know, deployments from

01:00:02.480 --> 01:00:07.089
[inaudible] customers took 4 - 5 months. The actual work was

01:00:07.109 --> 01:00:11.259
less, but the elapsed time to get them to set up the IT

01:00:11.279 --> 01:00:13.279
environments, secure the servers, get everything set up,

01:00:13.279 --> 01:00:18.609
they had to launch an IT project, and then we had to do a

01:00:18.629 --> 01:00:21.379
bunch of on premise stuff. It took about four or five

01:00:21.379 --> 01:00:24.869
months. We're doing deployments on platforms that we know in

01:00:24.889 --> 01:00:30.059
2 - 3 days now - literally from months to days.

01:00:30.079 --> 01:00:33.480
The 17 customers we had were all on a different version of

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:36.509
the code on

01:00:36.529 --> 01:00:39.190
[inaudible]. All of our Sass customers are on exactly the

01:00:39.210 --> 01:00:43.629
same code base and we do a weekly release. We do a weekly

01:00:43.649 --> 01:00:46.769
[inaudible] fix and feature update release every week. Every

01:00:46.789 --> 01:00:50.879
Thursday there's a release meeting where we push a new stack

01:00:50.899 --> 01:00:53.629
of code out every Friday every customer's on the same code

01:00:53.629 --> 01:00:54.629
base.

01:00:54.629 --> 01:00:57.690
And then fraud prevention, that's a big deal because the

01:00:57.710 --> 01:01:00.659
criminals are changing all the time. And we have a customer

01:01:00.679 --> 01:01:03.399
right now that we're trying to save that's still on

01:01:03.399 --> 01:01:05.000
[inaudible] who's on like four

01:01:05.019 --> 01:01:07.079
[inaudible] back because they haven't gone through the

01:01:07.079 --> 01:01:09.949
hassle of updating and they said, "You know, you're not

01:01:09.969 --> 01:01:13.480
stopping all the fraud." And we're like, "Well, 4.2 does.

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:16.699
That exact fraud case we stopped last week at another bank

01:01:16.719 --> 01:01:21.539
on 4.2. But you're on 3.0. We can't protect you with today's

01:01:21.559 --> 01:01:22.809
fraud with yesterday's code stack."

01:01:22.809 --> 01:01:27.500
So, having the Sass solution has been kind of revolutionary.

01:01:27.519 --> 01:01:31.349
And you know, there's an upfront fix cost, but every

01:01:31.369 --> 01:01:35.369
incremental customer that we add to the Sass solution, the

01:01:35.389 --> 01:01:39.049
marginal cost of that customer is very, very small. So, we

01:01:39.069 --> 01:01:41.569
can grow to a couple of hundred customers without really

01:01:41.579 --> 01:01:45.839
adding any significant fix costs for the business. And it

01:01:45.859 --> 01:02:15.859
becomes a very, very capital efficient model.

